IMPORT prelude

-- Sale of Goods (United Nations Convention) Act 1995 (Singapore) -- when the Vienna Convention (CISG) governs a sale (s 3, Schedule
-- Arts 1 to 6), formation of the contract (Arts 14, 16, 19), fundamental breach and the buyer's remedies (Arts 25, 39, 40, 44, 46, 49,
-- 50, 82), damages (Arts 75 to 77) and exemption for impediments (Art 79).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/SGUNCA1995.txt. The deposit says it "incorporates all
-- amendments up to and including 1 December 2021" and came into operation on 31 December 2021. Its Legislative History lists only
-- Act 14 of 1995 (commencement 1 March 1996) and the 1996 and 2013 Revised Editions: no amending Act is annotated.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0103 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by everyday-life
-- relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper
-- question yet. The Act itself is five sections; its substance is the Convention in the Schedule (101 articles). SCOPED to what a
-- Singapore business importing or exporting goods meets: does the Convention apply, is there a contract, may the buyer reject or
-- reduce the price, and what damages follow.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ss 1, 2, 3(3), 4 and 5 beyond their effect here; Arts 7 to 13 (interpretation, usages, place of business, form); Arts
-- 15, 17, 18, 20 to 24 (when communications take effect, time for acceptance); Arts 26 to 38 and 41 to 43 (seller's delivery and
-- conformity duties, third-party and IP claims); Arts 47, 48, 51, 52 (additional periods, seller's cure, partial and early or excess
-- delivery) except as inputs; Arts 53 to 65 (buyer's duties and seller's remedies) except Art 64's mirror in damages; Arts 66 to 70
-- (risk); Arts 71 to 73 (anticipatory breach, instalments); Art 74 (foreseeability cap) except as an input; Art 78 (interest -- the text
-- gives no rate); Arts 80, 81, 83 to 88 (effects of avoidance, preservation); Part IV (final provisions), which page-layout has partly
-- scrambled in the deposit (Art 92(2) runs into Art 99(6) in the extracted body; the full Arts 93 to 99 appear later in the raw text).
-- Which countries are Contracting States is a fact the Act leaves to Gazette notifications under s 5; none was retrieved.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill conventions (by way of the example rows). No
-- pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.

§ `Sale of Goods (United Nations Convention) Act 1995 -- sales`

GIVEN `amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the positive part` MEANS
    IF `amount` GREATER THAN 0 THEN `amount` ELSE 0

§§ `s 3 and Arts 1 to 6 -- when the Convention governs`

-- Art 1(1): "This Convention applies to contracts of sale of goods between parties whose places of business are in different States:
-- (a) when the States are Contracting States; or (b) when the rules of private international law lead to the application of the law
-- of a Contracting State." (2): the different-States fact "is to be disregarded whenever this fact does not appear either from the
-- contract or from any dealings between, or from information disclosed by, the parties".
-- s 3(2): "Sub-paragraph (1)(b) of Article 1 of the Convention does not have the force of law in Singapore and accordingly the
-- Convention will apply to contracts of sale of goods only between those parties whose places of business are in different States
-- when the States are Contracting States." (s 3(3) lets the Minister delete s 3(2) if the Art 95 reservation is withdrawn.)
-- Art 2: "This Convention does not apply to sales: (a) of goods bought for personal, family or household use, unless the seller ...
-- neither knew nor ought to have known that the goods were bought for any such use; (b) by auction; (c) on execution or otherwise by
-- authority of law; (d) of stocks, shares, investment securities, negotiable instruments or money; (e) of ships, vessels, hovercraft
-- or aircraft; (f) of electricity."
-- Art 3(1): goods to be manufactured are sales "unless the party who orders the goods undertakes to supply a substantial part of the
-- materials"; (2) not where "the preponderant part of the obligations of the party who furnishes the goods consists in the supply of
-- labour or other services".
-- Art 6: "The parties may exclude the application of this Convention".
DECLARE Sale HAS
    `places of business in different States` IS A BOOLEAN
    `that fact appears from the contract or dealings` IS A BOOLEAN
    `both States are Contracting States` IS A BOOLEAN
    `conflict rules point to a Contracting State's law` IS A BOOLEAN
    `bought for personal, family or household use` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller knew or ought to have known of that use` IS A BOOLEAN
    `sold by auction` IS A BOOLEAN
    `sold on execution or by authority of law` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a sale of shares, securities, negotiable instruments or money` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a sale of a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a sale of electricity` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer supplies a substantial part of the materials` IS A BOOLEAN
    `mainly labour or services` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties excluded the Convention` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the sale` IS A Sale
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the sale is outside the Convention by its nature` IF
        (`the sale`'s `bought for personal, family or household use` AND `the sale`'s `the seller knew or ought to have known of that use`)
    OR  `the sale`'s `sold by auction`
    OR  `the sale`'s `sold on execution or by authority of law`
    OR  `the sale`'s `a sale of shares, securities, negotiable instruments or money`
    OR  `the sale`'s `a sale of a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft`
    OR  `the sale`'s `a sale of electricity`
    OR  `the sale`'s `the buyer supplies a substantial part of the materials`
    OR  `the sale`'s `mainly labour or services`
    OR  `the sale`'s `the parties excluded the Convention`

GIVEN `the sale` IS A Sale
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Convention governs the sale in Singapore` IF
        `the sale`'s `places of business in different States`
    AND `the sale`'s `that fact appears from the contract or dealings`
    AND `the sale`'s `both States are Contracting States`
    AND (NOT `the sale is outside the Convention by its nature` `the sale`)

-- Counterfactual: the Convention as written, with Art 1(1)(b), which s 3(2) denies the force of law in Singapore.
GIVEN `the sale` IS A Sale
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Convention would govern without the section 3(2) reservation` IF
        `the sale`'s `places of business in different States`
    AND `the sale`'s `that fact appears from the contract or dealings`
    AND (`the sale`'s `both States are Contracting States` OR `the sale`'s `conflict rules point to a Contracting State's law`)
    AND (NOT `the sale is outside the Convention by its nature` `the sale`)

-- Art 4: the Convention "governs only the formation of the contract of sale and the rights and obligations of the seller and the
-- buyer"; "it is not concerned with: (a) the validity of the contract ...; (b) the effect which the contract may have on the property
-- in the goods sold." Art 5: it "does not apply to the liability of the seller for death or personal injury caused by the goods".
DECLARE Question IS ONE OF
    `whether a contract was formed`
    `whether the goods conform`
    `whether the contract is valid`
    `who owns the goods`
    `the seller's liability for personal injury caused by the goods`

GIVEN `the question` IS A Question
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Convention answers the question` IF
    CONSIDER `the question`
    WHEN `whether a contract was formed` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `whether the goods conform` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Arts 14, 16, 19 -- offer, revocation, reply`

-- Art 14(1): a proposal "addressed to one or more specific persons constitutes an offer if it is sufficiently definite and indicates
-- the intention of the offeror to be bound in case of acceptance. A proposal is sufficiently definite if it indicates the goods and
-- expressly or implicitly fixes or makes provision for determining the quantity and the price." (2): a proposal not so addressed "is
-- to be considered merely as an invitation to make offers, unless the contrary is clearly indicated".
-- INFERENCE: a public proposal that clearly indicates it is an offer must still be definite and show intention to be bound.
DECLARE Proposal HAS
    `addressed to specific persons` IS A BOOLEAN
    `clearly made as an offer to the public` IS A BOOLEAN
    `indicates the goods` IS A BOOLEAN
    `fixes or provides for the quantity` IS A BOOLEAN
    `fixes or provides for the price` IS A BOOLEAN
    `shows intention to be bound` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the proposal` IS A Proposal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposal is an offer` IF
        (`the proposal`'s `addressed to specific persons` OR `the proposal`'s `clearly made as an offer to the public`)
    AND `the proposal`'s `indicates the goods`
    AND `the proposal`'s `fixes or provides for the quantity`
    AND `the proposal`'s `fixes or provides for the price`
    AND `the proposal`'s `shows intention to be bound`

-- Art 16(1): "an offer may be revoked if the revocation reaches the offeree before he has dispatched an acceptance." (2): "However, an
-- offer cannot be revoked: (a) if it indicates, whether by stating a fixed time for acceptance or otherwise, that it is irrevocable;
-- or (b) if it was reasonable for the offeree to rely on the offer as being irrevocable and the offeree has acted in reliance".
GIVEN `revocation arrived before acceptance was dispatched` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the offer indicated it was irrevocable` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the offeree reasonably relied and acted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the revocation is effective` IF
        `revocation arrived before acceptance was dispatched`
    AND (NOT `the offer indicated it was irrevocable`)
    AND (NOT `the offeree reasonably relied and acted`)

-- Art 19(1): a reply "which purports to be an acceptance but contains additions, limitations or other modifications is a rejection of
-- the offer and constitutes a counter-offer." (2): terms "which do not materially alter the terms of the offer" make it an acceptance
-- "unless the offeror, without undue delay, objects". (3): terms relating "among other things, to the price, payment, quality and
-- quantity of the goods, place and time of delivery, extent of one party's liability to the other or the settlement of disputes are
-- considered to alter the terms of the offer materially." The list is open ("among other things"); `a non-material addition` stands
-- for whatever a tribunal finds immaterial.
DECLARE Modification IS ONE OF
    `no modification`
    `a different price`
    `different payment terms`
    `a different quantity`
    `a different time of delivery`
    `a cap on the seller's liability`
    `an arbitration clause`
    `a non-material addition`

GIVEN `the change` IS A Modification
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the change is material` IF
    CONSIDER `the change`
    WHEN `no modification` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a non-material addition` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the change` IS A Modification, `the offeror objected without undue delay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the reply is an acceptance` IF
    CONSIDER `the change`
    WHEN `no modification` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE (NOT `the change is material` `the change`) AND (NOT `the offeror objected without undue delay`)

§§ `Arts 25, 46, 49, 82 -- fundamental breach and avoidance`

-- Art 25: a breach "is fundamental if it results in such detriment to the other party as substantially to deprive him of what he is
-- entitled to expect under the contract, unless the party in breach did not foresee and a reasonable person of the same kind in the
-- same circumstances would not have foreseen such a result."
GIVEN `substantially deprives the other party` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the party in breach foresaw it` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a reasonable person would have foreseen it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the breach is fundamental` IF
        `substantially deprives the other party`
    AND (`the party in breach foresaw it` OR `a reasonable person would have foreseen it`)

-- Art 49(1): the buyer may declare the contract avoided "(a) if the failure by the seller ... amounts to a fundamental breach of
-- contract; or (b) in case of non-delivery, if the seller does not deliver the goods within the additional period of time fixed by the
-- buyer in accordance with paragraph (1) of article 47 or declares that he will not deliver within the period so fixed." (2): where the
-- seller has delivered, the buyer "loses the right to declare the contract avoided unless he does so" within a reasonable time.
-- Art 82(1): the buyer loses the right to avoid or to require substitute goods "if it is impossible for him to make restitution of the
-- goods substantially in the condition in which he received them"; (2) not if (a) that is not due to his act or omission, (b) the goods
-- perished or deteriorated through the Art 38 examination, or (c) they were sold in the normal course of business or consumed or
-- transformed in normal use before he discovered or ought to have discovered the lack of conformity.
-- Art 46(2): substitute goods "only if the lack of conformity constitutes a fundamental breach of contract and a request for substitute
-- goods is made either in conjunction with notice given under article 39 or within a reasonable time thereafter."
DECLARE `Buyer's position` HAS
    `the seller's breach is fundamental` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods were delivered` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer's additional period expired without delivery` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller said it would not deliver in that period` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer acted within a reasonable time` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the buyer can return the goods substantially as received` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the impossibility is not the buyer's doing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods deteriorated through examination` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods were resold or used up before the defect was discoverable` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the buyer` IS A `Buyer's position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `restitution does not bar the buyer` IF
        `the buyer`'s `the buyer can return the goods substantially as received`
    OR  `the buyer`'s `the impossibility is not the buyer's doing`
    OR  `the buyer`'s `the goods deteriorated through examination`
    OR  `the buyer`'s `the goods were resold or used up before the defect was discoverable`

GIVEN `the buyer` IS A `Buyer's position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may declare the contract avoided` IF
        (    `the buyer`'s `the seller's breach is fundamental`
         OR  (    (NOT `the buyer`'s `the goods were delivered`)
              AND (`the buyer`'s `the buyer's additional period expired without delivery` OR `the buyer`'s `the seller said it would not deliver in that period`)))
    AND ((NOT `the buyer`'s `the goods were delivered`) OR `the buyer`'s `the buyer acted within a reasonable time`)
    AND `restitution does not bar the buyer` `the buyer`

GIVEN `the buyer` IS A `Buyer's position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may require substitute goods` IF
        `the buyer`'s `the goods were delivered`
    AND `the buyer`'s `the seller's breach is fundamental`
    AND `the buyer`'s `the buyer acted within a reasonable time`
    AND `restitution does not bar the buyer` `the buyer`

§§ `Arts 39, 40, 44 -- notice of non-conformity`

-- Art 39(1): "The buyer loses the right to rely on a lack of conformity of the goods if he does not give notice to the seller
-- specifying the nature of the lack of conformity within a reasonable time after he has discovered it or ought to have discovered it."
-- (2): "In any event, the buyer loses the right ... if he does not give the seller notice thereof at the latest within a period of two
-- years from the date on which the goods were actually handed over to the buyer, unless this time-limit is inconsistent with a
-- contractual period of guarantee."
-- Art 40: "The seller is not entitled to rely on the provisions of articles 38 and 39 if the lack of conformity relates to facts of
-- which he knew or could not have been unaware and which he did not disclose to the buyer."
-- Art 44: "Notwithstanding the provisions of paragraph (1) of article 39 ..., the buyer may reduce the price in accordance with article
-- 50 or claim damages, except for loss of profit, if he has a reasonable excuse for his failure to give the required notice."
-- Time is counted in whole months here (two years = 24 months). INFERENCE: a contractual guarantee longer than two years is
-- "inconsistent" with the two-year limit, and notice within the guarantee period then suffices.
GIVEN `guarantee months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the outer limit for notice in months` MEANS
    IF `guarantee months` GREATER THAN 24 THEN `guarantee months` ELSE 24

GIVEN `notice within a reasonable time of discovery` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `months from handover to notice` IS A NUMBER,
      `guarantee months` IS A NUMBER,
      `the seller knew and did not disclose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may rely on the lack of conformity` IF
        `the seller knew and did not disclose`
    OR  (    `notice within a reasonable time of discovery`
         AND `months from handover to notice` AT MOST `the outer limit for notice in months` `guarantee months`)

-- Art 44 overrides only Art 39(1), the reasonable-time rule. It does not reach the two-year outer limit in Art 39(2).
GIVEN `notice within a reasonable time of discovery` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `months from handover to notice` IS A NUMBER,
      `guarantee months` IS A NUMBER,
      `the buyer has a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer keeps price reduction and damages other than lost profit` IF
        (NOT `notice within a reasonable time of discovery`)
    AND `the buyer has a reasonable excuse`
    AND `months from handover to notice` AT MOST `the outer limit for notice in months` `guarantee months`

§§ `Art 50 -- price reduction`

-- Art 50: the buyer "may reduce the price in the same proportion as the value that the goods actually delivered had at the time of the
-- delivery bears to the value that conforming goods would have had at that time. However, if the seller remedies any failure to perform
-- his obligations in accordance with article 37 or article 48 or if the buyer refuses to accept performance by the seller in accordance
-- with those articles, the buyer may not reduce the price." The reduction applies "whether or not the price has already been paid".
GIVEN `the seller cured under Article 37 or 48` IS A BOOLEAN, `the buyer refused the seller's cure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the buyer may reduce the price` IF
    (NOT `the seller cured under Article 37 or 48`) AND (NOT `the buyer refused the seller's cure`)

GIVEN `contract price` IS A NUMBER, `value if conforming` IS A NUMBER, `value as delivered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the reduced price` MEANS
    `contract price` TIMES `value as delivered` DIVIDED BY `value if conforming`

§§ `Arts 75 to 77 -- damages on avoidance`

-- Art 75: if the contract is avoided and, reasonably and within a reasonable time, "the buyer has bought goods in replacement or the
-- seller has resold the goods, the party claiming damages may recover the difference between the contract price and the price in the
-- substitute transaction as well as any further damages recoverable under article 74."
-- Art 76(1): with no substitute transaction, "the difference between the price fixed by the contract and the current price at the time
-- of avoidance ... If, however, the party claiming damages has avoided the contract after taking over the goods, the current price at
-- the time of such taking over shall be applied instead".
-- Art 77: if a party fails to mitigate, "the party in breach may claim a reduction in the damages in the amount by which the loss
-- should have been mitigated."
-- INFERENCE: "the difference" is recoverable only in the direction of loss (a buyer who covers more cheaply recovers nothing for it),
-- and damages do not go below zero. `further loss` is taken as already within the Art 74 foreseeability cap.
GIVEN `contract price` IS A NUMBER, `cover price` IS A NUMBER, `further loss` IS A NUMBER, `loss not mitigated` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the buyer's damages after buying in replacement` MEANS
    `the positive part` (`the positive part` (`cover price` MINUS `contract price`) PLUS `further loss` MINUS `loss not mitigated`)

GIVEN `contract price` IS A NUMBER, `resale price` IS A NUMBER, `further loss` IS A NUMBER, `loss not mitigated` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the seller's damages after reselling` MEANS
    `the positive part` (`the positive part` (`contract price` MINUS `resale price`) PLUS `further loss` MINUS `loss not mitigated`)

GIVEN `contract price` IS A NUMBER,
      `current price at avoidance` IS A NUMBER,
      `current price at taking over` IS A NUMBER,
      `avoided after taking over the goods` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the buyer's current-price damages` MEANS
    IF `avoided after taking over the goods`
    THEN `the positive part` (`current price at taking over` MINUS `contract price`)
    ELSE `the positive part` (`current price at avoidance` MINUS `contract price`)

§§ `Art 79 -- impediments`

-- Art 79(1): "A party is not liable for a failure to perform any of his obligations if he proves that the failure was due to an
-- impediment beyond his control and that he could not reasonably be expected to have taken the impediment into account at the time of
-- the conclusion of the contract or to have avoided or overcome it, or its consequences." (2): where the failure is that of a third
-- person engaged to perform, the party is exempt "only if: (a) he is exempt under the preceding paragraph; and (b) the person whom he
-- has so engaged would be so exempt". (3): only "for the period during which the impediment exists". (5): "Nothing in this article
-- prevents either party from exercising any right other than to claim damages" -- so avoidance and price reduction survive.
GIVEN `an impediment beyond its control` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `it could not reasonably have taken it into account` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `it could not have avoided or overcome it` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the failure was a subcontractor's` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the subcontractor would be exempt too` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party is exempt from damages` IF
        `an impediment beyond its control`
    AND `it could not reasonably have taken it into account`
    AND `it could not have avoided or overcome it`
    AND ((NOT `the failure was a subcontractor's`) OR `the subcontractor would be exempt too`)
